onic excitations  um due to the interaction with light and  in that case the ground stay born up  behind the classical dynamics is no  longer  going to accurately describe what's  happening  and additionally you can see similar  non-adiabatic phenomena  in scenarios like a molecule metal  interaction where in this case uh kind  of different to the  um the gas phase type photochemistry you  have um  many closely spaced potential energy  surfaces due to the like um the many  many electrons in the metal and the the  small spacing between the states can  lead to lots of like small excitations  where the motion  of the nuclei the translational  vibrational motion can couple to these  electron hole pair expectations  so  ideally we'd like to be able to treat  these with classical simulations because  we know these like scale very  effectively with um with the number of  degrees of freedom people have done  absolutely massive classical and dynamic  simulations like big biomolecules and  things like that however um  we need some way to to be able to  include the these electronic excitations  so one of the most popular ways of doing  this  are surface hopping methods  and these these involve running  classical dynamics on a single potential  energy surface the same way that you do  for classical molecular dynamics but  then when you enter coupling regions  um you can hop to different states and  this hopping  is based upon  integrating the time dependent  electronic schrodinger equation  alongside the the classical nuclear  dynamics you have coupled electron uh  nuclear motion and then when the  amplitudes become significantly large  you can hop to to other states and then  that's that's allows you to include  these non-adiabatic effects  then  in addition to this surf stopping  methods you they're also mean field type  methods or air infest and these  similarly to surface hopping you will  integrate the  electronic schrodinger equation  alongside the classical dynamics but 